{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348338063,"sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_f28246ab19ed4a95bd3852bc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-cannbench-kernel\">\n# Skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel\n\n# pyasc v2 CANNBench kernel workflow\n\nUse this skill only for the vendored benchmark under\n`integrations/cannbench/`. Its compact module contract overrides the generic\nstandalone-kernel workflow.\n\n## Non-negotiable contract\n\n- Treat `tasks/<op>/proto.yaml`, `golden.py`, and all 20 entries in\n  `cases.yaml` as one indivisible specification.\n- Produce one `candidate.py` with the exact top-level callable and defaults.\n- Use `torch` only for allocation, metadata views, and launches. Numerical work\n  belongs in one or more `@asc2.jit` kernels.\n- Import and call `ensure_npu_platform` from `._pyasc_runtime`.\n- Target `Ascend950PR_9599`, at most 72 vector cores, and UB capacity 253952 B.\n- For the pinned runtime use `asc2.global_tensor`, `asc2.copy_in`, and\n  `asc2.copy_out`; use `real_shape` for tails. Do not substitute the older\n  `tensor/load/store` spelling.\n- A production candidate normally uses bare `@asc2.jit` or an evidenced\n  allocation option. `always_compile=True` is a development aid, not a\n  submission requirement.\n\n## Required workflow\n\n1. Read the three task files and summarize every dtype, rank, attribute, list\n   arity, special value, and boundary shape. Do not optimize for one example.\n2. Read [operator patterns](references/operator-patterns.md), selecting only\n   the target operator section.\n3. Design host dispatch, kernel specializations, tails, dtype conversions, UB\n   budget, and numerical behavior before writing code.\n4. Implement the exact callable. Run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` and\n   the worker static contract check.\n5. Run the exact-v2 local compile gate for that operator as described in\n   [local validation](references/local-validation.md). A compile failure is\n   measured feedback; repair it and re-run all 20 cases.\n6. Have a different available model review the task, design, candidate, and\n   local report. Record both model IDs, OpenCode session IDs, loaded skill\n   source hashes, prompt/design/candidate hashes, and gate results.\n\nNever claim numerical correctness or performance from the local compile gate.\nOnly camodel execution can provide local numerical evidence; only CANNBench on\nthe real NPU is the acceptance/performance oracle.\n\n## Evidence labels\n\nUse exactly one label for every conclusion:\n\n- `verified-local-compile`: all task routes lower through pinned pyasc v2.\n- `verified-camodel`: outputs were executed and compared to the task golden.\n- `verified-cannbench`: official hardware result and case count are recorded.\n- `suspected`: evidence is incomplete or the root cause is inferred.\n\nDo not promote a locally qualified candidate into the canonical submission\npackage without numerical evidence. Do not spend a submission credit unless\nthe user explicitly enables remote evaluation.\n\n## Known limitations\n\nBefore using a workaround, consult\n[pyasc v2 blockers](references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md). Preserve full case\ncoverage when a case exposes a compiler limit; never remove or shrink the case\nto make a gate pass.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/local-validation.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel","dir":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel","time":{"start":1788348338050,"end":1788348338061}},"id":"prt_061de0ec4001CZRw7cSmAbohTX","sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","messageID":"msg_061ddfe7a001Y4PFFkOKthEtB4"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348338426,"sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_cd798ef86a664318b2d90163","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-syntax-constraints\">\n# Skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints\n\n# pyasc Syntax Constraints\n\nSyntax and keyword support varies across v2 snapshots. For CANNBench, the\nsource of truth is `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source` at the commit in\n`SOURCE_COMMIT`; load `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` and verify uncertain constructs\nagainst that source plus the exact-v2 compile gate. In particular, its\n`asc2.range` supports `unroll_factor` and `gm_barrier`, but not `parallel`.\n\n## Overview\n\npyasc compiles Python code to Ascend C via JIT. Only a subset of Python syntax is supported inside `@asc2.jit`-decorated functions. This skill documents what is and is not allowed.\n\n## Supported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Example | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| Attribute access | `nums.__len__()` | |\n| `while` loop | `while i < n: ...` | |\n| `for` loop | `for i in asc2.range(n): ...` | Use `asc2.range()` inside kernel, not `range()` |\n| Binary operators | `a + b * c` | `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `//`, `**`, `&`, `\\|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` |\n| Unary operators | `~a`, `-a`, `not a` | |\n| `tuple` | `return x, y, z` | |\n| `list` | `nums = [a, b, c]` | |\n| `pass` | `pass` | |\n| Ternary `if` | `ans = x if x > 0 else 0` | |\n| `if`/`elif`/`else` | Full conditional blocks | |\n| Constants | `a = 1; b = True` | |\n| Comparisons | `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=` | |\n| Function calls | `func()` | Must be `@asc2.jit`-decorated or asc2 built-in |\n| Boolean logic | `and`, `or` | |\n| Augmented assign | `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=` | |\n| Assignment | `x = expr` | |\n| Annotated assign | `count: int = 3` | |\n| f-strings + assert | `assert cond, f\"msg {val}\"` | |\n| Subscript | `nums[0]` | |\n| Slice | `tensor[offset:]` | |\n\n## Unsupported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Status | Workaround |\n|--------|--------|------------|\n| Nested functions | Not supported | Define separate `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `global` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| `return` from kernel | Not supported | Kernels write to output tensors; device functions may return |\n| `return` inside `if`/`for` | Not supported | Use top-level return only in device functions |\n| `continue` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `print()` | Not supported | Use `assert` with f-strings |\n| `with open(...)` | Not supported | Do file I/O outside JIT |\n| `raise` | Not supported | Use `assert` instead |\n| `try`/`except` | Not supported | Handle errors outside JIT |\n| `yield` / `yield from` | Not supported | No generators |\n| `lambda` | Not supported | Define named functions |\n| `break` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `import` / `from...import` | Not supported | All imports outside JIT |\n| `async def` / `async with` | Not supported | No async |\n| `nonlocal` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| Class methods as kernels | Not supported | Use module-level functions |\n| If-scoped variables | Restricted | Initialize before `if`; use `asc.ConstExpr` for compile-time branching |\n| Unsigned int comparison | Restricted | Use `np.int64` or truthiness check instead |\n\n## Type Constraints\n\n### Kernel parameter types (runtime)\n\n| Supported | Not supported |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| `bool`, `int`, `float` | `str` |\n| `numpy` scalars and `ndarray` | `tuple`, `list`, `dict` |\n| `torch.Tensor` (NPU mode only) | Complex objects |\n| `asc.GlobalAddress` | |\n\n### Compile-time parameters\n\nUse `asc.ConstExpr[T]` for values that must be resolved at compile time:\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    # tile_size is resolved at compile time and included in the JIT cache key\n    ...\n```\n\n### Built-in whitelist inside JIT\n\nOnly these built-ins are available: `dict`, `float`, `int`, `isinstance`, `issubclass`, `len`, `list`, `range`, `repr`, `str`, `tuple`, `type`\n\nNote: For iteration inside `@asc2.jit`, use `asc2.range()` instead of `range()`.\nDo not assume optional keywords are portable between pyasc snapshots.\n\n### Cube (matmul) loop + placement notes\n\n- **M/N/K tile loops are compile-time.** When the trip count is an\n  `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m/n/k tiles), a plain Python `for i in range(...)`\n  is fully unrolled at JIT time — that is the intended form for the loop that must\n  keep an operand resident in `L0A`/`L0B`. Use `asc2.range(...,\n  unroll_factor=2)` only on the *inner* tile loop you want software-pipelined\n  (double-buffered).\n- **Overlap (`gm_barrier=False`) doubles the pipelined L0 buffer.** The 2-deep\n  tile must fit half the L0 capacity (≤ 32 KiB for L0A/L0B, ≤ 64 KiB for the f32\n  L0C). A full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is 64 KiB and overflows when doubled\n  (`L0B overflow: 65536 bytes available, 131072 used`); shrink the tile (e.g.\n  `N_TILE=64`) before relying on overlap.\n- **Cube tile placement is explicit:** load/`asc2.copy` operands to\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L0A` (left) / `L0B` (right); stage from GM via\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L1` for reuse; the `@` result lands in L0C as f32 — cast with\n  `.to(asc2.float16)` before `asc2.store` if a 16-bit output is wanted.\n\n## Kernel vs Device Function Rules\n\n| Rule | Kernel function | Device function |\n|------|----------------|-----------------|\n| Launched from | Host via `kernel[core_num](...)` | Other `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `return` value | Not allowed | Allowed |\n| `@asc2.jit(...)` options | Effective (always_compile, etc.) | Ignored |\n| Nesting | Cannot be nested | Cannot be nested |\n\n## References\n\n- [Supported Syntax Details](references/supported-syntax.md)\n- [Unsupported Syntax Details](references/unsupported-syntax.md)\n- [Type Constraints](references/type-constraints.md)\n- [ConstExpr Guide](references/constexpr-guide.md)\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/supported-syntax.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/type-constraints.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/constexpr-guide.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/unsupported-syntax.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints","dir":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints","time":{"start":1788348338419,"end":1788348338425}},"id":"prt_061de10510013cRocW7ZIDCSAE","sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","messageID":"msg_061ddfe7a001Y4PFFkOKthEtB4"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348339246,"sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_fad1702599d244bb81794f57","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/swi_glu-generate/task.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/swi_glu-generate/task.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n2: \n3: # Task\n4: \n5: Operator: **SwiGlu**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `swi_glu` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n6: \n7: # Operator specification\n8: \n9: ## desc.md\n10: \n11: # SwiGlu 算子 API 描述\n12: \n13: ## 1. 算子简介\n14: \n15: 标准 SwiGLU 激活函数(Shazeer 2020 \"GLU Variants Improve Transformer\")。输入在指定 dim 上拆分为 x0 / x1 两等份,x0 经 SiLU 激活(`Swish_1(x) = x · sigmoid(x)`)后与 x1 做门控乘法。\n16: \n17: **主要应用场景**：\n18: - LLM FFN 中的 SwiGLU 门控(Llama / PaLM / Gemma 等)\n19: - 替代传统 GLU 与 ReLU FFN,提供更平滑的梯度与更强表达力\n20: \n21: **算子特征**：\n22: - 难度等级：L1（Elementwise）\n23: - P2 op：torch 无同名接口;reference 实现取自 `torch_npu.npu_swiglu` / ACLNN `aclnnSwiGlu`,两者都固定 Swish 的 β = 1(即 SiLU)\n24: - 单输入单输出,沿指定 dim 拆分 + 元素级运算\n25: \n26: ## 2. 算子定义\n27: \n28: ### 数学公式\n29: \n30: $$\n31: \\text{output} = \\text{SiLU}(x_0) \\odot x_1 = \\left( x_0 \\cdot \\sigma(x_0) \\right) \\odot x_1\n32: \\quad \\text{其中} \\quad (x_0, x_1) = \\text{chunk}(input, 2, \\text{dim})\n33: $$\n34: \n35: `σ(·)` 为 sigmoid;`⊙` 为逐元素乘法。\n36: \n37: > **注**:历史上 Swish 有可调参数 β(`Swish_β(x) = x · sigmoid(β·x)`),但 SwiGLU 在文献与主流实现(Llama / PaLM / torch_npu / aclnnSwiGlu)中**统一固定 β = 1**(等价 SiLU)。本 spec 不暴露 β,与 reference 实现严格对齐。\n38: \n39: ## 3. 接口规范\n40: \n41: ### 算子原型\n42: \n43: ```python\n44: cann_bench.swi_glu(Tensor input, int dim=-1) -> Tensor output\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ### 输入参数说明\n48: \n49: | 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n50: |------|------|--------|------|\n51: | input | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量,dim 维 size 必须是偶数 |\n52: | dim | int64 | -1 | 拆分维度 |\n53: \n54: ### 输出\n55: \n56: | 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n57: |------|-------|-------|------|\n58: | output | 与 input 同 rank,dim 维 size 折半 | 与 input 相同 | SwiGLU 激活输出 |\n59: \n60: ### 数据类型\n61: \n62: | 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n63: |-----------|-----------|\n64: | float16  | float16  |\n65: | float32  | float32  |\n66: | bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n67: \n68: ### 规则与约束\n69: \n70: - input 在 dim 维上 size 必须是偶数(否则无法等分 x0 / x1)\n71: - `dim` 支持负数索引(如 -1 表示最后一维)\n72: - output dtype 与 input 一致\n73: - FP16 / BF16 输入内部计算时升精度到 FP32,再 cast 回原 dtype(与 ACLNN 一致)\n74: \n75: ### 支持范围\n76: \n77: | 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n78: |---|---|---|\n79: | `rank(input)` | 2 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 2 ~ 5 维 |\n80: | 每个维度大小 `dim_i` | ≥ 2 (split 维需偶) | cases.csv 实测最大 1,000,003 |\n81: | 张量总元素数 | 1 ~ 2^30 | cases.csv 实测最大约 134M (8192×16384) |\n82: | `dim` | -rank(input) ~ rank(input)-1 | cases.csv 实测 dim=-1 / 0 / 1 / 2 |\n83: \n84: ## 4. 精度要求\n85: \n86: 采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n87: \n88: **误差指标**:\n89: \n90: 1. 平均相对误差(MERE):采样点中相对误差平均值\n91: 2. 最大相对误差(MARE):采样点中相对误差最大值\n92: \n93: **通过标准**:\n94: \n95: | 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 |\n96: |----------|---------|----------|---------|\n97: | **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 |\n98: \n99: 当 MERE < Threshold,MARE < 10 × Threshold 时判定为通过。\n100: \n101: \n102: ## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n103: \n104: ```python\n105: import torch\n106: import torch.nn.functional as F\n107: \n108: \n109: def swi_glu(input: torch.Tensor, dim: int = -1) -> torch.Tensor:\n110:     \"\"\"标准 SwiGLU 激活的 Torch Golden 参考实现 (P2 op).\n111: \n112:     公式: output = silu(x0) * x1 = (x0 * sigmoid(x0)) * x1\n113:     其中 x0, x1 = input.chunk(2, dim=dim).\n114:     \"\"\"\n115:     out_dtype = input.dtype\n116:     x = input.to(torch.float)\n117:     x0, x1 = x.chunk(2, dim=dim)\n118:     output = F.silu(x0) * x1\n119:     return output.to(out_dtype)\n120: ```\n121: \n122: ## 6. 额外信息\n123: \n124: ### 算子调用示例\n125: \n126: ```python\n127: import torch\n128: import cann_bench\n129: \n130: x = torch.randn(1024, 2048, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\n131: y = cann_bench.swi_glu(x, dim=-1)         # 输出 shape [1024, 1024]\n132: \n133: x = torch.randn(8192, 16384, dtype=torch.float16, device=\"npu\")\n134: y = cann_bench.swi_glu(x, dim=0)          # 输出 shape [4096, 16384]\n135: ```\n136: \n137: ## proto.yaml\n138: \n139: ```yaml\n140: operator:\n141:   name: SwiGlu\n142:   category: Elementwise\n143:   difficulty: L1\n144:   formula: output = silu(x0) * x1 = (x0 * sigmoid(x0)) * x1\n145:   description: 标准 SwiGLU 激活——输入在指定 dim 上等分成 x0 / x1,对 x0 做 SiLU (Swish_1),再与 x1 逐元素相乘\n146:   shape_support: 单输入,在指定 dim 上拆分成两等份;该 dim 的 size 必须是偶数\n147:   attrs:\n148:   - name: dim\n149:     type: int64\n150:     description: 拆分维度,默认为最后一维\n151:     required: false\n152:     default: -1\n153:   inputs:\n154:   - name: input\n155:     description: 输入张量,会在 dim 上拆分成 x0 / x1 两等份\n156:     dtype:\n157:     - float16\n158:     - float32\n159:     - bfloat16\n160:   outputs:\n161:   - name: output\n162:     description: 输出张量,shape 同 input 但 dim 维大小折半\n163:     dtype:\n164:     - float16\n165:     - float32\n166:     - bfloat16\n167:   schema: swi_glu(Tensor input, int dim=-1) -> Tensor output\n168: ```\n169: \n170: ## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n171: \n172: ```python\n173: #!/usr/bin/python3\n174: # coding=utf-8\n175: \n176: \n177: import torch\n178: import torch.nn.functional as F\n179: \n180: \n181: def swi_glu(input: torch.Tensor, dim: int = -1) -> torch.Tensor:\n182:     \"\"\"标准 SwiGLU 激活的 Torch Golden 参考实现 (P2 op).\n183: \n184:     公式: output = silu(x0) * x1 = (x0 * sigmoid(x0)) * x1\n185:     其中 x0, x1 = input.chunk(2, dim=dim).\n186: \n187:     标准 SwiGLU (Shazeer 2020 / Llama / PaLM) 固定 Swish 的 beta = 1\n188:     (即等价于 SiLU),没有可调 beta 参数。aclnnSwiGlu / torch_npu.npu_swiglu\n189:     也是同样定义,本 spec 与之对齐。\n190: \n191:     Args:\n192:         input: 输入张量,dim 维度上 size 必须是偶数\n193:         dim: 拆分维度,默认 -1\n194: \n195:     Returns:\n196:         output: 与 input 同 dtype/shape 但 dim 维度大小减半\n197:     \"\"\"\n198:     # FP16/BF16 升精度计算,与 ACLNN 内部一致.\n199:     out_dtype = input.dtype\n200:     x = input.to(torch.float)\n201:     x0, x1 = x.chunk(2, dim=dim)\n202:     output = F.silu(x0) * x1\n203:     return output.to(out_dtype)\n204: ```\n205: \n206: ## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n207: \n208: | case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n209: |---|---|---|---|---|\n210: | 1 | [[1024, 2048]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 1] | {'dim': -1} |\n211: | 2 | [[2048, 4096]] | ['float32'] | [-2, 2] | {'dim': -1} |\n212: | 3 | [[4096, 8192]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | {'dim': -1} |\n213: | 4 | [[8192, 16384]] | ['float16'] | [-10, 10] | {'dim': 0} |\n214: | 5 | [[2039, 65520]] | ['float32'] | [-100, 100] | {'dim': -1} |\n215: | 6 | [[1022, 2047]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'dim': 0} |\n216: | 7 | [[1009, 2016]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 2] | {'dim': -1} |\n217: | 8 | [[1538, 1537]] | ['float32'] | [-5, 10] | {'dim': 0} |\n218: | 9 | [[363, 367, 14]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50, 100] | {'dim': -1} |\n219: | 10 | [[2049, 1024]] | ['float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | {'dim': 1} |\n220: | 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 1018]] | ['float32'] | [-88, 88] | {'dim': -1} |\n221: | 12 | [[1000003, 2]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | {'dim': 1} |\n222: | 13 | [[11, 13, 16, 67]] | ['float32'] | [nan, nan] | {'dim': 2} |\n223: | 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1012]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | {'dim': -1} |\n224: | 15 | [[512, 4096]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | {'dim': -1} |\n225: | 16 | [[254, 16383]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1, 3] | {'dim': 0} |\n226: | 17 | [[4096, 1023]] | ['float16'] | [-1000, 1000] | {'dim': 0} |\n227: | 18 | [[2, 1023, 4096]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | {'dim': -1} |\n228: | 19 | [[4, 510, 4097]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | {'dim': 1} |\n229: | 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 512, 100]] | ['float32'] | [-20, 40] | {'dim': -1} |\n230: \n231: # Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n232: \n233: ```python\n234: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n235: \n236: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n237:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n238:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n239:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n240:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n241:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n242:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n243:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n244:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n245:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n246: \"\"\"\n247: \n248: import torch\n249: \n250: import asc\n251: import asc2\n252: \n253: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n254: \n255: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n256: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n257: _MAX_CORES = 72\n258: \n259: \n260: @asc2.jit\n261: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n262:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n263:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n264:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n265:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n266:                         unroll_factor=2):\n267:         off = t * tile_size\n268:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n269:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n270:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n271:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n272:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n273: \n274: \n275: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n276:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n277:     ensure_npu_platform()\n278:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n279:         x = x.contiguous()\n280:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n281:     size = x.numel()\n282:     if size == 0:\n283:         return out\n284:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n285:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n286:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n287:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n288:     else:\n289:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n290:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n291:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n292:     return out\n293: \n294: ```\n295: \n296: # pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n297: \n298: ## Module shape\n299: \n300: Your file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n301: \n302: - imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n303:   `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n304: - one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n305: - ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n306: - wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n307:   needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n308:   `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n309:   launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n310:   return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n311: \n312: ## Kernel authoring rules\n313: \n314: - Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n315:   `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n316:   copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n317: - Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n318:   (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n319:   value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n320: - Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n321: \n322: ```python\n323: for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n324:     off = t * tile_size\n325:     n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n326:     x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n327:     ...compute on tiles...\n328:     asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n329: ```\n330: \n331: - Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n332:   on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n333: - Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n334:   `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n335:   `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n336:   `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n337:   comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n338:   `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n339:   `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n340:   `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n341:   `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n342:   `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n343:   `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n344: - int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n345:   (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n346:   no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n347: - Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n348:   write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n349: - f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n350:   compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n351: - UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n352:   Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n353:   doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n354:   calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n355:   `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n356:   311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n357:   `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n358:   Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n359:   512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n360:   bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n361:   cases).\n362: - `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n363:   (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n364: - Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n365:   `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n366:   (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n367:   different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n368:   `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n369:   `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n370:   TypeError).\n371: - Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n372:   [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n373:   int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n374:   destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n375:   Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n376:   `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n377:   kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n378: - Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n379:   `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n380:   style (min 32 bytes).\n381: - Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n382:   `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n383:   `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n384: \n385: ## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n386: \n387: - Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n388:   nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n389:   `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n390: - Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n391:   - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n392:   - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n393:   - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n394:     `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n395:     for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n396:   - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n397:     `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n398:     the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n399: - IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n400:   through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n401:   unless the golden does.\n402: \n403: ## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n404: \n405: - ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n406: - torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n407:   `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n408:   `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n409:   `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n410: - FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n411:   `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n412:   `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n413:   `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n414:   rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n415:   is detected and scored as cheating.\n416: - Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n417:   shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n418: \n419: \n420: # Operator-specific guidance\n421: \n422: - input splits into x0 / x1 halves along attr dim (cases use dim 0, 1, 2 and\n423:   -1; that dim is always even). output = silu(x0) * x1 where\n424:   silu(v) = v * sigmoid(v); use the stable sigmoid form.\n425: - Zero-copy strided access (do NOT call .contiguous()/.narrow() to\n426:   materialize x0/x1 — extra device copies destroy the perf score): on the\n427:   host compute outer = prod(shape[:dim]), C = shape[dim],\n428:   inner = prod(shape[dim+1:]), half_cols = (C // 2) * inner. The contiguous\n429:   input viewed as 2-D [outer, C * inner] has x0 in columns [0, half_cols)\n430:   and x1 in columns [half_cols, 2 * half_cols) of every row. Kernel: 2-D\n431:   global tensors (runtime row/col counts are fine), loop over (row,\n432:   col-chunk) with [1, TILE] tiles and real_shape tails; output is\n433:   [outer, half_cols].\n434: - Distribute work over rows AND column-chunks so small-outer cases still use\n435:   many cores (e.g. grid-stride over row * num_col_tiles + col_tile).\n436: - MEASURED FAILURE to avoid: 2-D copy tiles require the LAST dimension to be\n437:   32-byte aligned. Case 12 is [1000003, 2] bf16 with dim=1 -> half_cols = 1\n438:   element = 2 bytes -> \"RuntimeError: Last dimension of tensor must be\n439:   aligned by 32 bytes, got 1 x 2 bytes\". Add a host-side fallback for\n440:   degenerate layouts: when `half_cols * input.element_size() < 32`, split\n441:   with metadata ops (which the anti-cheat explicitly allows):\n442:   `x0 = input.narrow(dim, 0, C // 2).contiguous()` and\n443:   `x1 = input.narrow(dim, C // 2, C // 2).contiguous()`, then run a plain\n444:   1-D elementwise silu-mul kernel over x0/x1. The two extra copy kernels\n445:   cost perf only on that one case; all aligned cases must keep the\n446:   zero-copy 2-D path.\n447: \n448: # Deliverable\n449: \n450: Write ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `swi_glu` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n451: \n452: IMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.\n\n(End of file - total 452 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **SwiGlu**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `swi_glu` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# SwiGlu 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\n标准 SwiGLU 激活函数(Shazeer 2020 \"GLU Variants Improve Transformer\")。输入在指定 dim 上拆分为 x0 / x1 两等份,x0 经 SiLU 激活(`Swish_1(x) = x · sigmoid(x)`)后与 x1 做门控乘法。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- LLM FFN 中的 SwiGLU 门控(Llama / PaLM / Gemma 等)\n- 替代传统 GLU 与 ReLU FFN,提供更平滑的梯度与更强表达力\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/swi_glu-generate/task.md","text":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **SwiGlu**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `swi_glu` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# SwiGlu 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\n标准 SwiGLU 激活函数(Shazeer 2020 \"GLU Variants Improve Transformer\")。输入在指定 dim 上拆分为 x0 / x1 两等份,x0 经 SiLU 激活(`Swish_1(x) = x · sigmoid(x)`)后与 x1 做门控乘法。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- LLM FFN 中的 SwiGLU 门控(Llama / PaLM / Gemma 等)\n- 替代传统 GLU 与 ReLU FFN,提供更平滑的梯度与更强表达力\n\n**算子特征**：\n- 难度等级：L1（Elementwise）\n- P2 op：torch 无同名接口;reference 实现取自 `torch_npu.npu_swiglu` / ACLNN `aclnnSwiGlu`,两者都固定 Swish 的 β = 1(即 SiLU)\n- 单输入单输出,沿指定 dim 拆分 + 元素级运算\n\n## 2. 算子定义\n\n### 数学公式\n\n$$\n\\text{output} = \\text{SiLU}(x_0) \\odot x_1 = \\left( x_0 \\cdot \\sigma(x_0) \\right) \\odot x_1\n\\quad \\text{其中} \\quad (x_0, x_1) = \\text{chunk}(input, 2, \\text{dim})\n$$\n\n`σ(·)` 为 sigmoid;`⊙` 为逐元素乘法。\n\n> **注**:历史上 Swish 有可调参数 β(`Swish_β(x) = x · sigmoid(β·x)`),但 SwiGLU 在文献与主流实现(Llama / PaLM / torch_npu / aclnnSwiGlu)中**统一固定 β = 1**(等价 SiLU)。本 spec 不暴露 β,与 reference 实现严格对齐。\n\n## 3. 接口规范\n\n### 算子原型\n\n```python\ncann_bench.swi_glu(Tensor input, int dim=-1) -> Tensor output\n```\n\n### 输入参数说明\n\n| 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n|------|------|--------|------|\n| input | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量,dim 维 size 必须是偶数 |\n| dim | int64 | -1 | 拆分维度 |\n\n### 输出\n\n| 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n|------|-------|-------|------|\n| output | 与 input 同 rank,dim 维 size 折半 | 与 input 相同 | SwiGLU 激活输出 |\n\n### 数据类型\n\n| 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| float16  | float16  |\n| float32  | float32  |\n| bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n\n### 规则与约束\n\n- input 在 dim 维上 size 必须是偶数(否则无法等分 x0 / x1)\n- `dim` 支持负数索引(如 -1 表示最后一维)\n- output dtype 与 input 一致\n- FP16 / BF16 输入内部计算时升精度到 FP32,再 cast 回原 dtype(与 ACLNN 一致)\n\n### 支持范围\n\n| 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `rank(input)` | 2 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 2 ~ 5 维 |\n| 每个维度大小 `dim_i` | ≥ 2 (split 维需偶) | cases.csv 实测最大 1,000,003 |\n| 张量总元素数 | 1 ~ 2^30 | cases.csv 实测最大约 134M (8192×16384) |\n| `dim` | -rank(input) ~ rank(input)-1 | cases.csv 实测 dim=-1 / 0 / 1 / 2 |\n\n## 4. 精度要求\n\n采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n\n**误差指标**:\n\n1. 平均相对误差(MERE):采样点中相对误差平均值\n2. 最大相对误差(MARE):采样点中相对误差最大值\n\n**通过标准**:\n\n| 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 |\n|----------|---------|----------|---------|\n| **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 |\n\n当 MERE < Threshold,MARE < 10 × Threshold 时判定为通过。\n\n\n## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\n\ndef swi_glu(input: torch.Tensor, dim: int = -1) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"标准 SwiGLU 激活的 Torch Golden 参考实现 (P2 op).\n\n    公式: output = silu(x0) * x1 = (x0 * sigmoid(x0)) * x1\n    其中 x0, x1 = input.chunk(2, dim=dim).\n    \"\"\"\n    out_dtype = input.dtype\n    x = input.to(torch.float)\n    x0, x1 = x.chunk(2, dim=dim)\n    output = F.silu(x0) * x1\n    return output.to(out_dtype)\n```\n\n## 6. 额外信息\n\n### 算子调用示例\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport cann_bench\n\nx = torch.randn(1024, 2048, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\ny = cann_bench.swi_glu(x, dim=-1)         # 输出 shape [1024, 1024]\n\nx = torch.randn(8192, 16384, dtype=torch.float16, device=\"npu\")\ny = cann_bench.swi_glu(x, dim=0)          # 输出 shape [4096, 16384]\n```\n\n## proto.yaml\n\n```yaml\noperator:\n  name: SwiGlu\n  category: Elementwise\n  difficulty: L1\n  formula: output = silu(x0) * x1 = (x0 * sigmoid(x0)) * x1\n  description: 标准 SwiGLU 激活——输入在指定 dim 上等分成 x0 / x1,对 x0 做 SiLU (Swish_1),再与 x1 逐元素相乘\n  shape_support: 单输入,在指定 dim 上拆分成两等份;该 dim 的 size 必须是偶数\n  attrs:\n  - name: dim\n    type: int64\n    description: 拆分维度,默认为最后一维\n    required: false\n    default: -1\n  inputs:\n  - name: input\n    description: 输入张量,会在 dim 上拆分成 x0 / x1 两等份\n    dtype:\n    - float16\n    - float32\n    - bfloat16\n  outputs:\n  - name: output\n    description: 输出张量,shape 同 input 但 dim 维大小折半\n    dtype:\n    - float16\n    - float32\n    - bfloat16\n  schema: swi_glu(Tensor input, int dim=-1) -> Tensor output\n```\n\n## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/python3\n# coding=utf-8\n\n\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\n\ndef swi_glu(input: torch.Tensor, dim: int = -1) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"标准 SwiGLU 激活的 Torch Golden 参考实现 (P2 op).\n\n    公式: output = silu(x0) * x1 = (x0 * sigmoid(x0)) * x1\n    其中 x0, x1 = input.chunk(2, dim=dim).\n\n    标准 SwiGLU (Shazeer 2020 / Llama / PaLM) 固定 Swish 的 beta = 1\n    (即等价于 SiLU),没有可调 beta 参数。aclnnSwiGlu / torch_npu.npu_swiglu\n    也是同样定义,本 spec 与之对齐。\n\n    Args:\n        input: 输入张量,dim 维度上 size 必须是偶数\n        dim: 拆分维度,默认 -1\n\n    Returns:\n        output: 与 input 同 dtype/shape 但 dim 维度大小减半\n    \"\"\"\n    # FP16/BF16 升精度计算,与 ACLNN 内部一致.\n    out_dtype = input.dtype\n    x = input.to(torch.float)\n    x0, x1 = x.chunk(2, dim=dim)\n    output = F.silu(x0) * x1\n    return output.to(out_dtype)\n```\n\n## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n\n| case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [[1024, 2048]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 1] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 2 | [[2048, 4096]] | ['float32'] | [-2, 2] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 3 | [[4096, 8192]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 4 | [[8192, 16384]] | ['float16'] | [-10, 10] | {'dim': 0} |\n| 5 | [[2039, 65520]] | ['float32'] | [-100, 100] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 6 | [[1022, 2047]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | {'dim': 0} |\n| 7 | [[1009, 2016]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 2] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 8 | [[1538, 1537]] | ['float32'] | [-5, 10] | {'dim': 0} |\n| 9 | [[363, 367, 14]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50, 100] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 10 | [[2049, 1024]] | ['float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | {'dim': 1} |\n| 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 1018]] | ['float32'] | [-88, 88] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 12 | [[1000003, 2]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | {'dim': 1} |\n| 13 | [[11, 13, 16, 67]] | ['float32'] | [nan, nan] | {'dim': 2} |\n| 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1012]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 15 | [[512, 4096]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 16 | [[254, 16383]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1, 3] | {'dim': 0} |\n| 17 | [[4096, 1023]] | ['float16'] | [-1000, 1000] | {'dim': 0} |\n| 18 | [[2, 1023, 4096]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | {'dim': -1} |\n| 19 | [[4, 510, 4097]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | {'dim': 1} |\n| 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 512, 100]] | ['float32'] | [-20, 40] | {'dim': -1} |\n\n# Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n\n```python\n\"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n\nKernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n  - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n  - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n  - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n    (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n  - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n    the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n    maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n  - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n    limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport torch\n\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\nfrom ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n\n_WIDE_TILE = 3072\n_NARROW_TILE = 1024\n_MAX_CORES = 72\n\n\n@asc2.jit\ndef _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                    size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n    out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n                        unroll_factor=2):\n        off = t * tile_size\n        n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n        x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n        xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n        y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n        asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n\n\ndef sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    if not x.is_contiguous():\n        x = x.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0:\n        return out\n    if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n    else:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n    return out\n\n```\n\n# pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n\n## Module shape\n\nYour file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n\n- imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n  `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n- one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n- ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n- wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n  needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n  `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n  launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n  return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n\n## Kernel authoring rules\n\n- Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n  `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n  copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n- Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n  (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n  value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n- Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n\n```python\nfor t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n    off = t * tile_size\n    n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n    x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n    ...compute on tiles...\n    asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n```\n\n- Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n  on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n- Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n  `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n  `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n  `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n  comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n  `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n  `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n  `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n  `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n  `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n  `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n- int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n  (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n  no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n- Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n  write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n- f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n  compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n- UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n  Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n  doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n  calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n  `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n  311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n  `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n  Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n  512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n  bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n  cases).\n- `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n  (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n- Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n  `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n  (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n  different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n  `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n  `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n  TypeError).\n- Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n  [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n  int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n  destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n  Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n  `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n  kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n- Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n  `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n  style (min 32 bytes).\n- Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n  `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n  `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n\n## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n\n- Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n  nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n  `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n- Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n  - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n  - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n  - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n    `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n    for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n  - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n    `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n    the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n- IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n  through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n  unless the golden does.\n\n## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n\n- ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n- torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n  `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n  `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n  `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n- FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n  `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n  `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n  `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n  rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n  is detected and scored as cheating.\n- Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n  shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n\n\n# Operator-specific guidance\n\n- input splits into x0 / x1 halves along attr dim (cases use dim 0, 1, 2 and\n  -1; that dim is always even). output = silu(x0) * x1 where\n  silu(v) = v * sigmoid(v); use the stable sigmoid form.\n- Zero-copy strided access (do NOT call .contiguous()/.narrow() to\n  materialize x0/x1 — extra device copies destroy the perf score): on the\n  host compute outer = prod(shape[:dim]), C = shape[dim],\n  inner = prod(shape[dim+1:]), half_cols = (C // 2) * inner. The contiguous\n  input viewed as 2-D [outer, C * inner] has x0 in columns [0, half_cols)\n  and x1 in columns [half_cols, 2 * half_cols) of every row. Kernel: 2-D\n  global tensors (runtime row/col counts are fine), loop over (row,\n  col-chunk) with [1, TILE] tiles and real_shape tails; output is\n  [outer, half_cols].\n- Distribute work over rows AND column-chunks so small-outer cases still use\n  many cores (e.g. grid-stride over row * num_col_tiles + col_tile).\n- MEASURED FAILURE to avoid: 2-D copy tiles require the LAST dimension to be\n  32-byte aligned. Case 12 is [1000003, 2] bf16 with dim=1 -> half_cols = 1\n  element = 2 bytes -> \"RuntimeError: Last dimension of tensor must be\n  aligned by 32 bytes, got 1 x 2 bytes\". Add a host-side fallback for\n  degenerate layouts: when `half_cols * input.element_size() < 32`, split\n  with metadata ops (which the anti-cheat explicitly allows):\n  `x0 = input.narrow(dim, 0, C // 2).contiguous()` and\n  `x1 = input.narrow(dim, C // 2, C // 2).contiguous()`, then run a plain\n  1-D elementwise silu-mul kernel over x0/x1. The two extra copy kernels\n  cost perf only on that one case; all aligned cases must keep the\n  zero-copy 2-D path.\n\n# Deliverable\n\nWrite ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `swi_glu` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n\nIMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":452,"totalLines":452,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/swi_glu-generate/task.md","time":{"start":1788348339237,"end":1788348339243}},"id":"prt_061de1163001Q7N4n1VYNhcxm8","sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","messageID":"msg_061ddfe7a001Y4PFFkOKthEtB4"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788348339273,"sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","part":{"id":"prt_061de1445001pEjMKwYC12UxOJ","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"","messageID":"msg_061ddfe7a001Y4PFFkOKthEtB4","sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":9201,"input":4725,"output":132,"reasoning":120,"cache":{"write":0,"read":4224}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788348341349,"sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","part":{"id":"prt_061de1c63001UaipHpjbiZN0GH","messageID":"msg_061de14490015D0fsw4E74Yevw","sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","snapshot":"","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348387130,"sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_9c09c2d145d94cfcb71aabb4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/swi_glu-generate/design.md","content":"# Design: SwiGlu kernel module (`candidate.py`)\n\n## Algorithm\n\nSwiGLU(input, dim) = SiLU(x0) * x1 where (x0, x1) = chunk(input, 2, dim).\nSiLU(v) = v * sigmoid(v). Sigmoid uses the overflow-safe form:\n  `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s,0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\nAll arithmetic lifted to f32 internally; final cast back to input dtype.\n\n## Dispatch: two paths (host decides before launch)\n\n### Path A — zero-copy 2-D strided (fast, all aligned cases)\n\nHost computes:\n  `outer = prod(shape[:dim])`, `C = shape[dim]`, `inner = prod(shape[dim+1:])`\n  `half_inner = (C // 2) * inner`\n  `row_bytes_aligned = half_inner * element_size >= 32`\n\nView input as contiguous `[outer, C * inner]` and output as `[outer, half_inner]`.\nx0 lives at columns `[0, half_inner)` and x1 at `[half_inner, 2*half_inner)` of\nevery row. The kernel does a 2-D tile (rows=1, cols=TILE) grid-stride over both\nrow and column-chunk so that even cases with outer=1 saturate all 72 cores.\n\n### Path B — fallback 1-D elementwise (degenerate last-dim alignment)\n\nTriggered when `half_inner * element_size < 32` (only case 12: `[1000003,2] bf16\ndim=1` -> half_inner=1 elem=2B). Host uses metadata-only view ops to produce\ncontiguous x0/x1 (`input.narrow(dim, 0, C//2).contiguous()` etc.), then a 1-D\ngrid-stride kernel over flat `size = outer * half_inner` elements.\n\nAnti-cheat permits `.narrow` + `.contiguous()` (metadata + view ops); these\ntwo extra copy-kernel launches cost perf only on case 12.\n\n## Case-by-case route map\n\n| Case | Shape | dtype | dim | Route | Notes |\n|------|-------|-------|-----|-------|-------|\n| 1 | [1024,2048] | f16 | -1 | A | half_inner=1024, aligned |\n| 2 | [2048,4096] | f32 | -1 | A | half_inner=2048, aligned |\n| 3 | [4096,8192] | bf16 | -1 | A | half_inner=4096, aligned |\n| 4 | [8192,16384] | f16 | 0 | A | outer=1, C=8192, inner=16384, half_inner=67108864 |\n| 5 | [2039,65520] | f32 | -1 | A | half_inner=32760, aligned |\n| 6 | [1022,2047] | bf16 | 0 | A | outer=1, C=1022, inner=2047, half_inner=511, aligned (511*2=1022>=32) |\n| 7 | [1009,2016] | f16 | -1 | A | half_inner=1008, aligned |\n| 8 | [1538,1537] | f32 | 0 | A | outer=1, C=1538, inner=1537, half_inner=769, aligned |\n| 9 | [363,367,14] | bf16 | -1 | A | half_inner=7, 7*2=14<32 -> B |\n| 10 | [2049,1024] | f16 | 1 | A | half_inner=512, aligned |\n| 11 | [3,7,13,1018] | f32 | -1 | A | half_inner=509, aligned (509*4=2036>=32) |\n| 12 | [1000003,2] | bf16 | 1 | **B** | half_inner=1, 1*2=2<32, alignment fail known |\n| 13 | [11,13,16,67] | f32 | 2 | A | outer=143, C=16, inner=67, half_inner=536, aligned |\n| 14 | [3,7,11,13,1012] | f16 | -1 | A | half_inner=506, aligned |\n| 15 | [512,4096] | f32 | -1 | A | half_inner=2048, aligned |\n| 16 | [254,16383] | bf16 | 0 | A | outer=1, C=254, inner=16383, half_inner=8191, aligned |\n| 17 | [4096,1023] | f16 | 0 | A | outer=1, C=4096, inner=1023, aligned |\n| 18 | [2,1023,4096] | f32 | -1 | A | half_inner=2048, aligned |\n| 19 | [4,510,4097] | bf16 | 1 | A | outer=4, C=510, inner=4097, half_inner=1023850, aligned |\n| 20 | [2,3,17,512,100] | f32 | -1 | A | half_inner=50, aligned |\n\nWait — case 9: half_inner=7, 7*2=14 < 32 -> needs Path B. Let me recheck.\nShape [363,367,14], dim=-1 -> dim=2, C=14, half_inner=7 for the inner=1. So\nhalf_inner=7, 7*2=14<32 -> Path B.\n\nRecorrected: Path B for cases 9 and 12 only.\n\n## Pinned-v2 APIs used\n\n- `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [outer, C_inner])` — 2-D view for Path A; `[size]` for Path B.\n- `asc2.copy_in(gm, [row_off, col_off], [1, TILE], real_shape=[1, n])` — tile load.\n- `asc2.copy_out(tile, gm, [row_off, col_off], real_shape=[1, n])` — tile store.\n- `asc2.range(start, stop, step, unroll_factor=2)` — grid-stride loop.\n- `.to(asc.float32)` / `.to(input_dtype)` — dtype promotion/demotion.\n- `asc2.exp`, `asc2.abs`, `asc2.minimum`, tile ops `+ - *` / `.to()` casts.\n- `asc2.where(cond, a, b)` — for the stable-sigmoid branch (if needed) or\n  elementwise blending.\n- Scalar-on-right arithmetic: `x * 0.5`, `x + 1.0`, never `0.5 * x`.\n- `asc.ConstExpr[int]` for TILE; plain `int` for runtime size/offset params.\n\n## Tiling strategy\n\n**Path A (2-D):** TILE_COLS = 1024 (f32 chain: ~10 visible f32 values * 4B * 1024 * 2 =\n81920 naive; *1.6 = 131072, well under 253952 UB budget). Grid-stride over\nflat job-id = row * num_col_tiles + col_tile, cores = min(72, total_tiles).\n\n**Path B (1-D):** TILE = 2048 (short chain, fewer intermediates). Grid-stride\nover tiles, cores = min(72, ceildiv(size, TILE)).\n\nTail handling: `n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off` on the\ncolumn axis, fed to `real_shape=[1, n]` (or `[n]` for 1-D).\n\n## UB budget estimate\n\nSiLU*mul chain: load x0(f16), load x1(f16), x0_f32, x1_f32, neg_x0,\nexp_min_x0 (after abs+min), abs_x0, exp_negabs, sigmoid_num, sigmoid_den,\nsigmoid_val, silu_val, mul_out = ~13 f32 tile slots visible.\nNaive: 13 * 4 * 1024 * 2 = 106496. * 1.6 factor = 170394 B < 253952 B. OK at TILE=1024.\nAt TILE=2048: 13 * 4 * 2048 * 2 = 212992 naive. * 1.6 = 340787 -> OVERFLOW.\n=> **TILE_COLS = 1024 for Path A; TILE = 2048 for Path B (shorter chain).**\n\n## Numerical risks and mitigations\n\n| Risk | Source | Mitigation |\n|------|--------|------------|\n| exp overflow | x0 large positive (cases: ±100, ±65504, ±inf) | `exp(min(x0, 0))` form; min clamps before exp |\n| exp underflow | x0 large negative | exp(-inf) -> 0, numerically correct (sigmoid=0, silu=0) |\n| Division by zero | 1+exp(-|x|) -> always >= 1, safe | No risk |\n| Inf propagation | case 12 has [±inf, ±inf] bf16 | IEEE: exp(min(inf,0))=1, exp(-inf)=0, sigmoid(inf)=1, silu(inf)=inf, inf*x1=±inf or nan. Matches golden. |\n| NaN propagation | case 13 (all-NaN input) | NaN through arithmetic -> NaN output. Matches golden (golden also produces NaN). |\n| Catastrophic cancellation | Not present: SiLU is `x*sigmoid(x)`, no subtraction of near-equal | No mitigation needed |\n| Small-value precision | case 14 (all zeros), case 6 ([-0.1,0.1]) | f32 internal compute; sigmoid(0)=0.5, silu(0)=0. Exact. |\n| BF16 rounding | 2^-7 threshold is generous | f32 internal compute satisfies this |\n\n## Anti-cheat compliance\n\n- All compute in `@asc2.jit` kernels — no torch math ops anywhere.\n- torch used only for: `ensure_npu_platform()`, `.shape`, `.numel()`,\n  `.element_size()`, `.dtype`, `.is_contiguous()`, `.contiguous()`,\n  `torch.empty(...)`, and metadata views (`.narrow()`) on Path B only.\n- Output allocated with `torch.empty(out_shape, dtype=..., device=input.device)`.\n- Output is a fresh contiguous tensor, not a view of input.\n\n## Local validation ladder\n\n1. **Static check:** `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` — syntax.\n2. **Contract check:** worker static contract checker against the module shape.\n3. **Exact-v2 compile gate:** run the pinned pyasc v2 compile for all 20 cases;\n   every case must lower through without UB overflow or codegen errors.\n   Label: `verified-local-compile` on full pass.\n4. **Never claim numerical correctness** from local gates; only camodel or\n   CANNBench on real NPU can.\n\n## Special-value handling\n\n- `dim` normalization: if negative, convert via `dim = dim + rank`. Always valid per spec.\n- All-zero input (case 14): silu(0)=0, output=0. No issue.\n- All-NaN input (case 13): NaN propagates through arithmetic -> NaN output.\n- Inf input (case 12): follows the IEEE-safe sigmoid path above.\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/swi_glu-generate/design.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/swi_glu-generate/design.md","time":{"start":1788348387123,"end":1788348387128}},"id":"prt_061de24de001S7bB6lUnCnrYSb","sessionID":"ses_f9e220253ffedV3OX4mgAXGzSL","messageID":"msg_061de14490015D0fsw4E74Yevw"}}
